      "recommended_risk": "low",
      "technical_explanation": "The scanner raised a low-confidence obfuscation signal, but the supplied source is plainly readable ES module JavaScript: standard SillyTavern extension imports, named functions, literal strings, Korean comments, and jQuery UI wiring. There is no eval or dynamic code execution, no encoded or hex-escaped string blobs, no packed or self-decoding content, and no concealed second-stage logic. The capabilities actually present — reading chat context, subscribing to generation and prompt events, splicing a user-configured prompt into the per-request message array, persisting prompt presets through the host's debounced settings save, and writing the in-memory execution log to the clipboard on a button click — each match the extension's stated purpose, remain local to the host, and are visible in the settings UI. The signal appears to be a false positive on ordinary template literals, regex usage, or non-ASCII comment text rather than evidence of obfuscation.",
      "layman_explanation": "An automated scanner guessed that part of this file might be deliberately scrambled or hidden code, but its guess had low confidence and reading the actual file shows ordinary, human-readable code with no hiding techniques. The extension does exactly what its description says: when you press send with an empty input box, it inserts your chosen prompt into that one request, and it keeps a local log you can copy. Nothing was found that hides what it does, sends data anywhere, or reaches beyond its stated purpose.",
